Simple web stack (one server) for www.foobar.com

Flow:
A user types www.foobar.com in the browser. The browser asks the DNS for
the IP of www.foobar.com. The www record is an A record pointing to
8.8.8.8. The browser opens a TCP/IP connection to 8.8.8.8 on port 80 and
sends an HTTP request. Nginx receives it, serves static files directly and
forwards dynamic requests to the application server, which runs the code
base and queries MySQL. The generated page goes back to the user via Nginx.

Components:
- Server: a physical or virtual machine that runs programs and provides
  services to other computers over a network.
- Domain name: a human-readable alias (foobar.com) for an IP address,
  resolved by the DNS.
- DNS record type of www: an A record (maps a hostname to an IPv4 address).
- Web server (Nginx): accepts HTTP requests, serves static content and
  forwards dynamic requests to the application server.
- Application server: executes the code base (business logic) and
  generates dynamic content.
- Database (MySQL): stores and retrieves the application's data.
- Communication with the user: TCP/IP over the Internet, using HTTP.

Issues:
- SPOF: a single server; if it goes down, the whole website is down.
- Downtime during maintenance: deploying new code or restarting the web
  server makes the site unavailable.
- Cannot scale: one server cannot handle a large traffic increase; there
  is nowhere to distribute the load.